{"level":"debug","message":"ToolResult created","result":{"output":"{\n  \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-solar\",\n  \"title\": \"Solar Energy Basics | NREL\",\n  \"content\": \"Solar Energy BasicsSolar energy is a powerful source of energy that can be used to heat, cool, and power our homes and businesses. Text versionMore energy from the sun falls on the Earth in one hour than is used by everyone in the world in one year. A variety of technologies convert sunlight to usable energy for buildings. The most commonly used solar technologies are solar photovoltaics for electricity, passive solar design for space heating and cooling, and solar water heating. Businesses and industry use solar technologies to diversify their energy sources, improve efficiency, and save money. Energy developers and utilities use solar photovoltaic and concentrating solar power technologies to produce electricity on a massive scale to power our electric grid. Solar electricity is one of the fastest growing sources of energy in the world. Solar Photovoltaic TechnologyConverts sunlight directly into electricity to power homes and businesses.Passive Solar TechnologyProvides light and harnesses heat from the sun to warm our homes and businesses in winter. Solar Water HeatingHarnesses heat from the sun to provide hot water for homes and businesses.Solar Process HeatUses solar energy to heat or cool commercial and industrial buildings.Concentrating Solar PowerHarnesses heat from the sun to provide electricity for large power stations.Additional ResourcesSolar ResearchSolar News and Feature StoriesHow Does Solar Work? (U.S. Department of Energy)Solar Energy Resources (U.S. Department of Energy)BuildingsNEXT™ Student Design Competition (U.S. Department of Energy)Energy Kids: Solar Basics (U.S. Energy Information Administration) Share Last Updated Aug. 27, 2025\",\n  \"clean_text\": \"Solar Energy BasicsSolar energy is a powerful source of energy that can be used to heat, cool, and power our homes and businesses. Text versionMore energy from the sun falls on the Earth in one hour than is used by everyone in the world in one year. A variety of technologies convert sunlight to usable energy for buildings. The most commonly used solar technologies are solar photovoltaics for electricity, passive solar design for space heating and cooling, and solar water heating. Businesses and industry use solar technologies to diversify their energy sources, improve efficiency, and save money. Energy developers and utilities use solar photovoltaic and concentrating solar power technologies to produce electricity on a massive scale to power our electric grid. Solar electricity is one of the fastest growing sources of energy in the world. Solar Photovoltaic TechnologyConverts sunlight directly into electricity to power homes and businesses.Passive Solar TechnologyProvides light and harnesses heat from the sun to warm our homes and businesses in winter. Solar Water HeatingHarnesses heat from the sun to provide hot water for homes and businesses.Solar Process HeatUses solar energy to heat or cool commercial and industrial buildings.Concentrating Solar PowerHarnesses heat from the sun to provide electricity for large power stations.Additional ResourcesSolar ResearchSolar News and Feature StoriesHow Does Solar Work? (U.S. Department of Energy)Solar Energy Resources (U.S. Department of Energy)BuildingsNEXT™ Student Design Competition (U.S. Department of Energy)Energy Kids: Solar Basics (U.S. Energy Information Administration) Share Last Updated Aug. 27, 2025\",\n  \"markdown\": \"Solar Energy BasicsSolar energy is a powerful source of energy that can be used to heat, cool, and power our homes and businesses. Text versionMore energy from the sun falls on the Earth in one hour than is used by everyone in the world in one year. A variety of technologies convert sunlight to usable energy for buildings. The most commonly used solar technologies are solar photovoltaics for electricity, passive solar design for space heating and cooling, and solar water heating. Businesses and industry use solar technologies to diversify their energy sources, improve efficiency, and save money. Energy developers and utilities use solar photovoltaic and concentrating solar power technologies to produce electricity on a massive scale to power our electric grid. Solar electricity is one of the fastest growing sources of energy in the world. Solar Photovoltaic TechnologyConverts sunlight directly into electricity to power homes and businesses.Passive Solar TechnologyProvides light and harnesses heat from the sun to warm our homes and businesses in winter. Solar Water HeatingHarnesses heat from the sun to provide hot water for homes and businesses.Solar Process HeatUses solar energy to heat or cool commercial and industrial buildings.Concentrating Solar PowerHarnesses heat from the sun to provide electricity for large power stations.Additional ResourcesSolar ResearchSolar News and Feature StoriesHow Does Solar Work? (U.S. Department of Energy)Solar Energy Resources (U.S. Department of Energy)BuildingsNEXT™ Student Design Competition (U.S. Department of Energy)Energy Kids: Solar Basics (U.S. Energy Information Administration) Share Last Updated Aug. 27, 2025\",\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"author\": \"\",\n    \"published_date\": \"\",\n    \"word_count\": 243,\n    \"language\": \"en\",\n    \"domain\": \"www.nrel.gov\",\n    \"content_type\": \"article\",\n    \"canonical_url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-solar\",\n    \"description\": \"\",\n    \"keywords\": []\n  },\n  \"extracted_data\": {\n    \"headings\": [\n      {\n        \"level\": 1,\n        \"text\": \"Solar Energy Basics\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"level\": 2,\n        \"text\": \"Solar Photovoltaic Technology\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"level\": 2,\n        \"text\": \"Passive Solar Technology\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"level\": 2,\n        \"text\": \"Solar Water Heating\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"level\": 2,\n        \"text\": \"Solar Process Heat\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"level\": 2,\n        \"text\": \"Concentrating Solar Power\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"level\": 2,\n        \"text\": \"Additional Resources\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"paragraphs\": [\n      \"Solar energy is a powerful source of energy that can be used to heat, cool, and power our homes and businesses.\",\n      \"More energy from the sun falls on the Earth in one hour than is used by everyone in\\n    the world in one year. A variety of technologies convert sunlight to usable energy\\n    for buildings. The most commonly used solar technologies are solar photovoltaics for electricity, passive solar design for space heating and\\n    cooling, and solar water heating.\",\n      \"Businesses and industry use solar technologies to diversify their energy sources,\\n    improve efficiency, and save money. Energy developers and utilities use solar photovoltaic\\n    and concentrating solar power technologies to produce electricity on a massive scale\\n    to power our electric grid. Solar electricity is one of the fastest growing sources of energy in the world.\",\n      \"Converts sunlight directly into electricity to power homes and businesses.\",\n      \"Provides light and harnesses heat from the sun to warm our homes and businesses in\\n    winter.\",\n      \"Harnesses heat from the sun to provide hot water for homes and businesses.\",\n      \"Uses solar energy to heat or cool commercial and industrial buildings.\",\n      \"Harnesses heat from the sun to provide electricity for large power stations.\",\n      \"BuildingsNEXT™ Student Design Competition (U.S. Department of Energy)\",\n      \"Energy Kids: Solar Basics (U.S. Energy Information Administration)\"\n    ],\n    \"key_points\": [],\n    \"quotes\": [],\n    \"links\": [\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Skip to main content\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-solar#content\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Research\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Text version\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/news/video/solar-energy-basics-text.html\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Solar Photovoltaic Technology\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-photovoltaics\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Passive Solar Technology\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-passive-solar\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Solar Water Heating\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-solar-water-heating\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Solar Process Heat\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-solar-process\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Concentrating Solar Power\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/research/re-csp\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Solar Research\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/solar\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Solar News and Feature Stories\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.nrel.gov/news/news?ign=1&search=solar&year=all\",\n        \"external\": false\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"How Does Solar Work?\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/how-does-solar-work\",\n        \"external\": true\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"Solar Energy Resources\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.energy.gov/eere/solar/solar-energy-resources\",\n        \"external\": true\n      },\n      {\n        \"text\": \"BuildingsNEXT™ Student Design Competition\",\n        \"url\": \"https://www.solardecathlon.gov/\",\n        \"external\": true\n      